id: k8s.deployment.rolling-update
kind: kubernetes
title: Rolling update
aliases: rolling deployment, rollout, zero downtime deploy
tags: deployment, availability
questions:
  - how would you deploy a service without downtime?
  - how do Kubernetes rolling updates work?
invariant: Maintain enough healthy replicas while replacing the old ReplicaSet.
answer_30s: Run two ReplicaSets at once: the new one grows as the old one shrinks, bounded by maxSurge and maxUnavailable, and only Pods that pass readiness join the Service.
answer_2m: |
  maxSurge bounds how many extra Pods exist above the desired count;
  maxUnavailable bounds how far below it you may go. The controller waits for
  new Pods to become Ready before scaling the old ReplicaSet down further, so
  capacity changes by at most one step at a time. Zero downtime additionally
  requires: a readiness probe that reflects real serving ability, graceful
  shutdown (SIGTERM + preStop + terminationGracePeriodSeconds), in-flight
  request draining, and a database migration strategy compatible with both
  versions running at the same time.
when_to_use:
  - stateless services
  - gradual application rollout
avoid_when:
  - the application cannot tolerate mixed versions in the same schema
commands:
  - kubectl rollout status deployment/api => is the new ReplicaSet healthy yet?
  - kubectl rollout history deployment/api => which revisions exist and what changed
  - kubectl rollout undo deployment/api => revert to the previous template
  - kubectl get rs -o wide => how far the shift old ReplicaSet -> new ReplicaSet has progressed
  - kubectl describe deployment/api => which maxSurge/maxUnavailable bounds are in force
failure_modes:
  - bad readiness probe
  - incompatible database migration
  - insufficient cluster capacity for surge
  - no graceful shutdown, so in-flight requests fail
misuse:
  - kubectl rollout restart deployment/api => restarting is not a rollout; it recycles Pods without changing the template
follow_ups:
  - how do you roll back when the new version broke the schema?
related:
  - k8s.deployment
  - k8s.replica-set
  - k8s.probe.readiness
  - design.graceful-shutdown
sources:
  - https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/controllers/deployment/#rolling-update-deployment
